#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301496 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "337be080-59df-51bf-b0d5-14dba139b9c0" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Horizontal Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d1e627b7-bfd6-534e-830d-90b90bf24d0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2=2{t}^{2},\\,y-2=2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301498} {:answer/id #uuid "6598d4bd-0f51-59f8-96b1-480f3601275c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2=2{t}^{2},\\,y-2=4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301499} {:answer/id #uuid "809636ff-d992-572c-a491-dbcaab56b899" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y+2=t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301500} {:answer/id #uuid "36b5d164-c361-542e-8465-94bac038821d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y-2=t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301501} {:answer/id #uuid "96e52685-a506-5142-9b93-9931257df6a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2=2{t}^{2},\\,y+2=4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301502}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6598d4bd-0f51-59f8-96b1-480f3601275c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2=2{t}^{2},\\,y-2=4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301499} :answer-field/id #uuid "6baca86a-7538-54f8-b930-9937030a4c0e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301497}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "84bc7a7c-9e6f-5cc0-a103-68903527aa4c" :question/math-academy-id "q-84840" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039044/assets/0d278fe1bf34cd60877be6886c90e1734fab7433f9f94ad7765d379e69c2da42.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(2,2),$ and passes through the point $(4,6).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(2,2),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx-2=p{t}^{2},\\,y-2=2pt.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(4,6),$ we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=6$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2=p{t}^{2} \\\\ 4=2pt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =2pt \\\\ 2 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{2}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =p{(\\frac{2}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 2 & =p\\cdot \\frac{4}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 2 & =\\frac{4}{p} \\\\ p & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-2=(2){t}^{2},\\,y-2=2(2)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-2=2{t}^{2},\\,y-2=4t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301503 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "337be080-59df-51bf-b0d5-14dba139b9c0" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Horizontal Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b76ff9a-20ce-58dd-bb5b-a2c91fe9986e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+4=-2{t}^{2},\\,y-2=-2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301505} {:answer/id #uuid "af9df707-eabb-52a0-bce2-cc41d484d781" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-4={t}^{2},\\,y+2=2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301506} {:answer/id #uuid "c88ca086-1050-5d05-ac5b-1586a1b40966" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+4=-{t}^{2},\\,y-2=-2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301507} {:answer/id #uuid "d9a45b32-3143-507e-adbd-6eae182996ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-4=-{t}^{2},\\,y+2=-2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301508} {:answer/id #uuid "fa501ae9-403a-52fc-b10a-a5abbc6d0417" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+4={t}^{2},\\,y-2=t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301509}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c88ca086-1050-5d05-ac5b-1586a1b40966" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+4=-{t}^{2},\\,y-2=-2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301507} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc2f9049-4ae2-592c-b08f-435e027d0fcb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301504}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "23c1a911-6224-553e-84b2-80970be0d8e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-84843" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039044/assets/ba3d2c8719c97911d2339548f08601b07bcfe555d59dbb058a157836c45a34fe.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(-4,2),$ and passes through the point $(-5,4).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(-4,2),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx+4=p{t}^{2},\\,y-2=2pt.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-5,4),$ we can substitute $x=-5$ and $y=4$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-1=p{t}^{2} \\\\ 2=2pt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =2pt \\\\ 1 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & =p{(\\frac{1}{p})}^{2} \\\\ -1 & =p\\cdot \\frac{1}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ -1 & =\\frac{1}{p} \\\\ p & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx+4=(-1){t}^{2},\\,y-2=2(-1)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx+4=-{t}^{2},\\,y-2=-2t.\n$$"}]}
